        <skos:altLabel xml:lang="zh-cn">长岸和港口工人赔偿法索赔</skos:altLabel>
        <skos:definition>A claim made under the Longshore and Harbor Workers’ Compensation Act (LHWCA), which provides benefits to certain maritime workers who are injured or become ill while working on navigable waters or in adjoining areas used for loading, unloading, repairing, or building a vessel.</skos:definition>
    </owl:Class>
    


    <!-- https://folio.openlegalstandard.org/R9t8WXRLn7pookX0bFcOwqo -->

    <owl:Class rdf:about="https://folio.openlegalstandard.org/R9t8WXRLn7pookX0bFcOwqo">
        <rdfs:subClassOf rdf:resource="https://folio.openlegalstandard.org/RKY2B2HNSx5K82dgVN6Qn7"/>
        <rdfs:subClassOf>
            <owl:Restriction>
                <owl:onProperty rdf:resource="https://folio.openlegalstandard.org/RN3mWlXZUAt07dYsu6tls2"/>
                <owl:someValuesFrom rdf:resource="https://folio.openlegalstandard.org/R76UTCrg8DDzlFkZCPjrdqe"/>
            </owl:Restriction>
        </rdfs:subClassOf>
        <rdfs:label>Petition for Skilled Worker and Specialty Occupation Visa</rdfs:label>
        <skos:altLabel>H1B Petition</skos:altLabel>
        <skos:definition>The &quot;Petition for Specialty Occupation Visa&quot; generally refers to the H-1B visa petition in the United States. The H-1B visa is a non-immigrant visa category that allows U.S. employers to hire foreign workers in specialty occupations that require specialized knowledge and skills. The employer must file a Form I-129 petition with U.S. Citizenship and Immigration Services (USCIS), demonstrating that the position meets the criteria of a specialty occupation and that the beneficiary (the foreign worker) has the necessary qualifications. If approved, the beneficiary can work in the U.S. for the sponsoring employer for a specified period, typically up to three years with the possibility of extension</skos:definition>
    </owl:Class>
    


